Notes for the SEM–DSM hybrid method

1. Time integration scheme (LDDRK vs. Newmark):
The Newmark time integration scheme may introduce significant numerical noise, particularly at high frequencies. In SEM–DSM hybrid simulations, these artifacts can produce large errors that are typically confined to narrow frequency bands and appear as spurious oscillations in the synthetic waveforms. To mitigate this issue, either:

*use the Low-Dissipation and Low-Dispersion Runge–Kutta (LDDRK) time integration scheme, which substantially reduces high-frequency numerical noise but is computationally more expensive; or
*improve the SEM mesh quality (e.g., by refining or optimizing the element distribution), which can also alleviate the numerical artifacts while retaining the Newmark scheme.
